Service Management (Systemd)
• systemctl start <service> 	— Starts a background service.
• systemctl stop <service> 	— Stops a running service.
• systemctl restart <service> 	— Restarts a service (commonly used after changing a config file).
• systemctl enable <service> 	— Configures a service to start automatically upon system boot.
• systemctl status <service> 	— Checks whether a service is active, inactive, or failing.
• journalctl -u <service> --no-pager — Fetches the system logs specifically generated by that service.

Networking & Remote Server Management
• ssh user@ip_address 		— Connects securely to remote servers or cloud virtual machines.
• scp file.tar.gz user@ip:/path — Copies files securely between local and remote environments.
• curl -I https://example.com 	— Sends HTTP requests to test endpoints and verify if web applications are running properly.
• netstat -tuln or ss -tuln 	— Checks open ports and listening services to debug firewall or application binding issues.
• ping <host> 			— Tests basic network connectivity to another server.

Process Management & System Monitoring
• ps aux or ps -ef 	— Displays all active processes currently running on the server.
• top or htop 		— Provides an interactive, real-time view of system resources like CPU, memory, and running tasks.
• kill -9 <PID> 	— Forcefully terminates a malfunctioning or hung process using its Process ID.
• df -h 		— Displays disk space usage in a human-readable format.
• free -h 		— Displays system memory (RAM) usage.

Linux Filesystem Hierarchy
• / (Root) — The starting point of the entire directory tree.
• /etc — Configuration files for the system and applications (e.g., Nginx, SSH).
• /var/log — System and application logs; the primary destination for debugging.
• /home — Personal directories for regular users to store data and scripts.
• /root — The isolated home directory reserved for the administrator (root) user.
• /bin & /sbin — Essential system commands and binaries needed to run the OS.
• /opt — Third-party, optional software packages (e.g., GitLab, custom tools).
• /tmp — Temporary files that are automatically cleared upon system reboot.
• /proc & /sys — Virtual directories exposing kernel and process hardware data in real time.

Package Manager
A Package Manager is an automated tool that installs, updates, configures, and removes software on a Linux system.
Common Package Managers by Linux Family
Linux Distribution Family				High-Level Manager	Low-Level Tool	Package Format
Debian / Ubuntu / Mint					apt or apt-get		dpkg		.deb
RHEL / CentOS / Rocky Linux / Fedora			dnf or yum		rpm		.rpm
Alpine Linux (Popular for tiny Docker containers)	apk			apk		.apk

Essential Commands
• Update package list: 	sudo apt update  
• Install software: 	sudo apt install docker.io
• Upgrade installed 	apps: sudo apt upgrade
• Remove software: 	sudo apt remove nginx
